<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Refool</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14078v1/IAD.svg" id="S1.F1.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/358;" width="476" height="358"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">IAD</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14078v1/Narcissus.svg" id="S1.F1.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/358;" width="476" height="358"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Narcissus</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Robustness performance of state-of-the-art safety tuning strategies, including BTI-DBF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, ANP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu and Wang, 2021</a>)</cite>
and SAU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite> under various attack settings. The O-ASR metric denotes the attack success rate (ASR) under the original defense methods,
while the R-ASR indicates the ASR after applying the Retuning Attack. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the promising progress in safety tuning strategies, prior studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> show that their robustness remains limited,
particularly against adaptive attacks or those characterized by low orthogonality or low linearity. More alarmingly, growing evidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2024</a>)</cite>
suggests that these defenses may merely provide a false sense of security: the purified models still retain residual backdoor features that can be readily reactivated.
As illustrated by the Retuning Attack proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al. (2024)</a></cite>, the backdoor can be effectively reactivated by fine-tuning a purified model
for a few epochs using a dataset that contains only a small number of poisoned samples. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, although state-of-the-art
safety tuning methods achieve low attack success rates (ASR) against diverse backdoor attacks, they remain notably vulnerable to the Retuning Attack (RA).
When exposed to RA, the backdoor can be rapidly reactivated, causing the R-ASR (<em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> the ASR after applying RA) to surge above 80% in most cases, whereas clean models
exhibit strong resilience under the same attack settings. These observations indicate that existing safety tuning techniques fail to fundamentally remove embedded
backdoor features. Query-based Reactivation Attacks (QRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2024</a>)</cite> further confirm that even in a black-box setting, residual backdoors can be
efficiently reactivated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Most existing defense strategies focus on output-level robustness, addressing backdoor effects solely through prediction outcomes. Such approaches lack
a principled understanding of how backdoor knowledge is encoded in model parameters, leaving residual backdoor effects that make purified models vulnerable to
reactivation attacks. Although model reconstruction-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu and Wang, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2018</a>)</cite> attempt to directly identify
and remove backdoor-related parameters, they struggle to balance backdoor mitigation and model performance, particularly when clean and backdoor features are
entangled, as in attacks with low orthogonality or low linearity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we conceptualize backdoor effects as a form of task-specific memory embedded in model parameters, aiming to establish a theoretical framework
for deep backdoor memory removal while preserving clean task knowledge, which guides the design of backdoor unlearning methods. Continual learning (CL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thrun, 1995</a>)</cite>,
which studies memory retention and forgetting across sequential tasks, provides a natural theoretical foundation. It allows us to systematically analyze the dynamics
of backdoor forgetting through unlearning and derive foundational design principles. Compared with direct unlearning approaches, the forgetting perspective in
Continual learning avoids directly operating in the parameter space and instead provides a principled framework for selectively forgetting backdoor-specific memory
while preserving clean task knowledge through task alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Continual learning (CL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thrun, 1995</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Liu, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a setting in which an agent must learn from an incoming stream of data
throughout its lifetime. A major challenge is Catastrophic Forgetting (CF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirkpatrick et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bennani et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doan et al., 2021</a>)</cite>,
where learning a new task could abruptly degrade performance on previously learned tasks. Prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2019</a>)</cite> shows that forgetting is closely
tied to task similarity: higher alignment between tasks results in more severe CF. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doan et al. (2021)</a></cite> futher provide a general definition of CF and
introduce the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) overlap matrix as a measure of task similarity. Recently, Zhang <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">et al.</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> first formulate
backdoor learning as a continual learning problem, with two independent phases: ① an initial rapid learning phase in which the backdoor task is
acquired within just a few training epochs, followed by ② a subsequent phase of gradual adaptation to the clean task, which provides a novel perspective
for understanding backdoor attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by these studies, we build upon adversarial training and rethink backdoor adversarial unlearning through the lens of continual learning (CL).
Our goal is to establish a formal definition of backdoor unlearning based on the catastrophic forgetting (CF) mechanism<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bennani et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doan et al., 2021</a>)</cite>
and investigate its core challenges, particularly the fundamental principles that govern effective backdoor removal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">What conditions must an unlearning task satisfy to completely eliminate backdoor effects while preserving performance on clean tasks?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we address the above problem through the lens of catastrophic forgetting in continual learning. First, we uniformly consider the processes of
backdoor learning and backdoor unlearning as a stream of three-stage learning tasks <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{c},\tau_{b},\tau_{u}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{c},\tau_{b},\tau_{u}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of
a sequential order of clean task <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, backdoor task <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, and backdoor unlearning task <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we provide a formal definition of complete backdoor unlearning,
in which the backdoor effects are completely removed after the unlearning operation, and the resulting purified model achieves performance on the clean task
that is comparable to that of the clean model. Furthermore, we introduce a necessary condition that the unlearning task <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy to approximately
achieve complete backdoor unlearning: the unlearning task <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> should be aligned with the backdoor task <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> while remaining orthogonal to the clean task <math id="S1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, for the problem of backdoor adversarial unlearning, we propose a fundamental principle for crafting adversarial examples during adversarial training,
<em id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> the adversarial perturbation should closely align with the backdoor features while preserving the primary semantic content of the original sample.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, we formally cast backdoor adversarial unlearning as a blind inversion problem with a maximum a posteriori (MAP) objective. Furthermore, we integrate
a bi-level formulation of adversarial training within the Expectation-Maximization (EM) framework to effectively optimize the MAP<sub id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S1.p9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">θ</span></sub> objective.
Based on the above analysis, we propose a robust backdoor adversarial unlearning method, termed Blind Inversion-Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning (BI-BAU).
We further extend BI-BAU to untargeted adversarial scenarios with unknown target classes <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as to multi-modal contrastive learning settings, thereby
broadening its applicability in real-world scenarios. Extensive experiments demonstrate the universal effectiveness of BI-BAU against a wide range of backdoor attacks,
including those characterized by low orthogonality or low linearity. Moreover, BI-BAU exhibits strong post-purification robustness, maintaining a low ASR even
under backdoor re-activation attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, our main contributions are three-fold:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical foundation for backdoor unlearning.</span> To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to systematically investigate the fundamental
challenges of backdoor unlearning from the perspective of continual learning. By leveraging the catastrophic forgetting mechanism, we provide
a formal definition of complete backdoor unlearning and derive the necessary condition that an unlearning task must satisfy to achieve thorough removal
of backdoor effects.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Blind Inversion-Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning (BI-BAU).</span> Building on the theoretical insights, we propose BI-BAU, which formalizes
backdoor adversarial unlearning as a blind inversion problem with a <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MAP}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>MAP</mtext><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MAP}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> optimization objective. We solve this problem through a bi-level
adversarial training process integrated within the Expectation-Maximization (EM) framework. BI-BAU is further extended to untargeted attacks with unknown
target classes and to multi-modal contrastive learning scenarios, enhancing its applicability to realistic deployment settings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comprehensive robustness evaluation.</span> We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of existing backdoor attacks, demonstrating that our method is
broadly effective across a wide spectrum of attacks, including more challenging cases characterized by low orthogonality or low linearity,
while achieving near-complete removal of backdoor effects while maintaining high performance on clean tasks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Continual Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Continual learning has been introduced to meet the needs of intelligent systems to incrementally acquire, update, accumulate, and exploit knowledge throughout
their lifetime, enabling them to adapt to dynamic environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Continual learning is characterized by learning from dynamic data
distributions, where the model receives a stream of learning tasks with training samples drawn from different distributions.
Despite significant advancements in the field, continual learning remains explicitly constrained by catastrophic forgetting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">McCloskey and Cohen, 1989</a>)</cite>,
wherein learning a new task often leads to a severe degradation in performance on previously learned tasks.
In the context of continual learning, existing research on catastrophic forgetting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirkpatrick et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doan et al., 2021</a>)</cite> primarily focuses on mitigating
or avoiding its detrimental effects, such as the substantial reduction in the model’s ability to retain previous distributions when adapting to new ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, in contrast to studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirkpatrick et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doan et al., 2021</a>)</cite> that seek to overcome forgetting, we shift our perspective to investigate
its potential benefits. We aim to incorporate the problem of backdoor unlearning into the continual learning framework, so that we can leverage it as a theoretical
tool to analyze how backdoors can be effectively erased while preserving the model’s performance on clean tasks through the lens of catastrophic forgetting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Backdoor Attacks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gu <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">et al.</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2019</a>)</cite> pioneered backdoor attacks in deep learning with BadNets, initiating extensive research in this area. Early attacks were largely
sample-agnostic, using uniform triggers easily detected by synthesis-based defenses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. This prompted the emergence of
sample-specific backdoor attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen and Tran, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salem et al., 2022</a>)</cite> with varying triggers. To enhance stealthiness, various invisibility strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen and Tran, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020</a>)</cite>
have been proposed. However, dirty-label attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen and Tran, 2020</a>)</cite> remain detectable via label-image inconsistencies, clean-label attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turner et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>
circumvent this by aligning poisoned samples’ labels with target classes. In addition to the various forms of backdoor attacks, research has also delved into exploring
their underlying mechanisms. Existing work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geirhos et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> suggests that the effectiveness of backdoor attacks largely stems from
shortcut learning, which induces a <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latent separability</em> phenomenon: feature representations of poisoned samples tend to form distinct clusters that are
well separated from those of clean samples. This observation provides a key theoretical foundation upon which many existing backdoor defense methods are built.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, Qi <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">et al.</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2023</a>)</cite> challenge this assumption by proposing two adaptive attacks: Adaptive-Blend and Adaptive-Patch, as counter-examples.
Moreover, Zhang <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">et al.</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> formulate backdoor attacks as a continual learning task and introduce two key properties of backdoor attacks:
orthogonality and linearity. They conduct a systematic study on existing mainstream backdoor attacks and defenses and show that existing defenses are particularly
vulnerable to attacks with low orthogonality or low linearity. Collectively, these findings indicate that existing defenses offer only limited robustness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Backdoor Defenses</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Safety tuning strategies have been proposed to remove backdoor effects from a pre-deployed backdoor model, including trigger synthesis-based methods,
model reconstruction-based methods, and backdoor adversarial unlearning methods. Trigger synthesis-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>
reconstruct triggers via model inversion techniques and then unlearn the hidden backdoor, whereas model reconstruction-based approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu and Wang, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2018</a>)</cite>
prune backdoor-related neurons exhibiting abnormal sensitivities to poisoned samples and recover clean accuracy using a small clean set.
Backdoor adversarial unlearning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite> leverages adversarial training (AT) to enhance robustness,
though naive AT often incurs accuracy degradation due to training instability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weng et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Most existing work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite>
largely prioritize improving model performance on clean tasks, while offering only limited investigation into the core challenges of backdoor defense.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the promising progress made by existing defenses, recent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have revealed that current safety tuning techniques
merely provide a false sense of security. Residual backdoor features remain in so-called purified models and can be readily reactivated by adversaries through
sample-specific perturbations generated solely via model queries. Although the Path-Aware Minimization (PAM) method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2024</a>)</cite> achieves promising
results in deep backdoor removal, its reliance on BTI-DBF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for generating backdoor samples inherently limits its effectiveness against attacks
characterized by low orthogonality or low linearity, due to BTI-DBF’s constrained trigger reverse-engineering capability. Consequently, genuinely removing backdoors
remains a critical and unresolved challenge in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Preliminary</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Notation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_{0}}\times\mathbb{R}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_{0}}\times\mathbb{R}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the training set, with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\left\{x:(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\left\{x:(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\left\{y:(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\left\{y:(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> representing the input and label sets, respectively. In the context of backdoor attacks, we consider a <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-class (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>)
classification problem. The model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is implanted with a backdoor by training on a corrupted dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, which consists of both a clean subset <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and
a poisoned backdoor subset <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=D_{c}\cup D_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=D_{c}\cup D_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. The backdoor model misclassifies any input <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> carrying the backdoor trigger as the target label <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For clarity and without loss of generality, we consider a fully-connected feed-forward network <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=FC\circ f_{L}\circ f_{L-1}\circ...\circ f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=FC\circ f_{L}\circ f_{L-1}\circ...\circ f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> hidden layers,
where the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">l</span>-th layer is denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,...,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,...,L</annotation></semantics></math>, and FC denotes the fully-connected layers. Overall, the architecture of the model <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as
consisting of a feature extractor <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{backbone}}=f_{L}\circ f_{L-1}\circ\dots\circ f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>backbone</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{backbone}}=f_{L}\circ f_{L-1}\circ\dots\circ f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the Backbone Network) followed by a fully connected (FC) classification head.
For an input <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{l}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{l}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{l}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{l}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the pre-activation and post-activation representations at layer <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
In particular, we define the output of the feature extractor as <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(x)=f_{\text{backbone}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>backbone</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(x)=f_{\text{backbone}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The recurrence relation for a feed-forward network is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{cases}h^{l+1}=z^{l}w^{l+1}+b^{l+1}\\
z^{l+1}=\phi\left(h^{l+1}\right)\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{cases}h^{l+1}=z^{l}w^{l+1}+b^{l+1}\\
z^{l+1}=\phi\left(h^{l+1}\right)\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is a pointwise activation function, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{l+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{l}\times n_{l+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{l+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{l}\times n_{l+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{l+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{l+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{l+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{l+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the weight matrix
and bias vector, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In continual learning (CL), we encounter a sequence of supervised learning tasks <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the task identifier,
with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathbb{N}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathbb{N}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> = <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x^{\tau}_{j},y^{\tau}_{j})\}_{j=1}^{n_{\tau}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x^{\tau}_{j},y^{\tau}_{j})\}_{j=1}^{n_{\tau}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the dataset associated with task <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The objective is to learn a predictor <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{T}\to\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{T}\to\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> that achieves the prediction accuracy as high as possible.
In the CL setting, access to data from previous tasks is restricted unless explicitly stored in a memory buffer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lopez-Paz and Ranzato, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parisi et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.
We denote the Euclidean norm of a vector or the spectral norm of a matrix by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and use <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle\cdot,\cdot\right\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle\cdot,\cdot\right\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> to represent the Euclidean dot product.
The task index is denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, and learnable parameters by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the parameters during training on task <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, the symbol <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> represents the state of a variable at the end of a task, so <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\tau}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\tau}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the learned parameters upon completing task <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
A comprehensive list of all notations in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Summary of Symbols ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Threat Model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To formally study backdoor adversarial unlearning, we define the capabilities of the adversary and defender in a realistic deployment scenario.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adversary</span>. We consider a realistic deployment scenario in which practitioners primarily obtain pre-trained models from third-party platforms (<em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> HuggingFace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain, 2022</a>)</cite>)
to reduce training costs and accelerate downstream development. Despite their strong performance and practical convenience, such resource models may be compromised
during pre-training and thus contain latent backdoors. To ensure security and reliability, these models are typically subjected to safety tuning or purification
before being fine-tuned for downstream tasks or deployed in real-world systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we focus on post-purification security, motivated by recent evidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2024</a>)</cite> that existing safety tuning strategies
are insufficient to fundamentally eliminate backdoor behaviors. This allows these compromised models to be fine-tuned for downstream tasks while the backdoors persist.
We consider an adversary aiming to reactivate these residual backdoors following the defender’s application of safety tuning. Importantly, the adversary has no
access to the training data, purification procedure, or model parameters. Instead, the attacker operates in a query-only black-box setting, which closely reflects
practical deployment scenarios. Specifically, the adversary can only submit inputs to the post-purified model and observe the corresponding outputs.
Under this threat model, the attacker can launch Query-based Reactivation Attacks (QRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which exploit model queries to generate
sample-specific perturbations that trigger residual backdoor behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Defender</span>. Consistent with existing safety tuning approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, we assume the defender has access to a small clean
dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{cl}=\left\{(x_{i},y_{i})\right\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{cl}=\left\{(x_{i},y_{i})\right\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for post-hoc fine-tuning. We further define <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{-y_{t}}=\left\{(x_{i},y_{i})|(x_{i},y_{i})\in\mathcal{D}_{cl},y_{i}\neq y_{t}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{-y_{t}}=\left\{(x_{i},y_{i})|(x_{i},y_{i})\in\mathcal{D}_{cl},y_{i}\neq y_{t}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>
as the subset of these non-target samples. It is important to note that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{cl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{cl}</annotation></semantics></math> is limited, and the defender is unable to train a new model from scratch
based on <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{cl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{cl}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The defender’s objective is to eliminate the backdoor effects embedded in the suspicious model while preserving its performance on clean tasks.
To this end, we formulate backdoor adversarial unlearning as the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\theta}\mathcal{R}_{cl}+\mathcal{R}_{adv}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta}\mathcal{R}_{cl}+\mathcal{R}_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\begin{matrix}\underset{\theta}{\min}\;\mathcal{R}_{cl}=\underset{\theta}{\min}\;\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{cl}}(\ell(f(x;\theta),y)),\\
\underset{\theta}{\min}\;\mathcal{R}_{adv}=\underset{\theta}{\min}\;\underset{\delta\in S}{\max}\;\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{-y_{t}}}(\ell_{adv}(f(\tilde{x};\theta),y)).\end{matrix}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜃</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.447em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜃</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜃</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.447em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜃</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">S</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\begin{matrix}\underset{\theta}{\min}\;\mathcal{R}_{cl}=\underset{\theta}{\min}\;\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{cl}}(\ell(f(x;\theta),y)),\\
\underset{\theta}{\min}\;\mathcal{R}_{adv}=\underset{\theta}{\min}\;\underset{\delta\in S}{\max}\;\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{-y_{t}}}(\ell_{adv}(f(\tilde{x};\theta),y)).\end{matrix}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">The first term, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{cl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{cl}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2. Threat Model ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, represents the classification risk of the model on the clean dataset,
where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the model parameters, and the second term, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{adv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math>, refers to the adversarial risk. Adversarial training (AT) formulates a minimax
optimization problem to reduce this risk, where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}=x+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}=x+\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the adversarial example and the perturbation set <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>
characterizes the adversary’s manipulative capability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>NTK framework for Continual Learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2019)</a></cite> demonstrate that, under the NTK regime, neural networks evolve as a linear model:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\tau}^{*}(x)=f_{\tau-1}^{*}(x)+\langle\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x),\theta_{\tau}^{*}-\theta_{\tau-1}^{*}\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau}^{*}(x)=f_{\tau-1}^{*}(x)+\langle\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x),\theta_{\tau}^{*}-\theta_{\tau-1}^{*}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\tau}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\tau}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> being the final weight after training on task <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. The latter formulation implies the feature maps <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times p}</annotation></semantics></math>
are constant over time. Building on this framework, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bennani et al. (2020)</a></cite> showed that continual learning (CL) dynamics can be characterized as
a recursive kernel regression process. As a result, the time evolution of the linearized neural network can be analytically derived without running gradient descent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Catastrophic Forgetting Under the NTK Regime</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In line with the theoretical framework established by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doan et al. (2021)</a></cite>, we adopt their definitions of Catastrophic Forgetting under the Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) regime,
which serve as a basis for our analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Definition 0 (<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Drift</span>).</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>) be the source task (respectively target task), <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\tau_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\tau_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> the source test set, the CF of task <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> after training on all the subsequent tasks up to the target task <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})=\Bigl(f^{\star}_{\tau_{T}}(x)-f^{\star}_{\tau_{S}}(x)\Bigr)_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}_{\tau_{S}}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})=\Bigl(f^{\star}_{\tau_{T}}(x)-f^{\star}_{\tau_{S}}(x)\Bigr)_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}_{\tau_{S}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> represents prediction changes for inputs from the task <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> after learning task <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the classification setting, we consider the K-dimensional output of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\tau}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. To quantify the overall extent of forgetting on task <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we use the squared norm of this change vector.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Definition 0 (<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Catastrophic Forgetting</span>).</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>) be the source task (respectively target task), <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\tau_{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\tau_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> the source test set, the Catastrophic Forgetting of a
source task <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to a target task <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|\delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|\delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}_{\tau_{S}}}(f^{\star}_{\tau_{T}}(x)-f^{\star}_{\tau_{S}}(x))^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}_{\tau_{S}}}(f^{\star}_{\tau_{T}}(x)-f^{\star}_{\tau_{S}}(x))^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\theta_{\tau}^{\star},\forall\tau\in[T]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\theta_{\tau}^{\star},\forall\tau\in[T]\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the weight at the end of the training of task <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>,
based on Eq. <a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.3. NTK framework for Continual Learning ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the catastrophic forgetting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2019</a>)</cite> is further defined as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|\delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|\delta^{\tau_{S}\rightarrow\tau_{T}}(X^{\tau_{S}})\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\|\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(X^{\tau_{S}})(\theta_{\tau_{T}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{S}}^{*})\right\|_{2}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\|\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(X^{\tau_{S}})(\theta_{\tau_{T}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{S}}^{*})\right\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This linear formulation under the NTK regime provides clear insights into the dynamics of catastrophic forgetting.
This framework allows us to quantify how backdoor features can be forgotten or preserved across tasks, which is critical for designing effective backdoor
unlearning strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we first unify backdoor learning and subsequent unlearning within a continual learning framework, introduce
a formal definition of complete backdoor unlearning, and further derive the key condition for achieving it.
Building on this, Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Blind Inversion ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> casts the problem as a blind inversion problem and proposes a solution based on maximum a posteriori estimation
using the Expectation-Maximization (EM) algorithm. Finally, in section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Proposed Method ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we propose Blind Inversion-Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning (BI-BAU)
and extend it to the more general settings, including untargeted adversarial training and multimodal contrastive learning scenarios.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021b</a>)</cite> views backdoor learning as a composition of a clean task <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and a backdoor task <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite> further
characterize this process as a two-stage task flow: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{b},\tau_{c}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{b},\tau_{c}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of an initial rapid learning phase of the backdoor task <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>
within a few training epochs followed by a subsequent phase of gradually learning over the clean task <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. They also observe that the gradients from the two tasks
tend to be orthogonal, indicating an orthogonality between the backdoor and clean task. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doan et al. (2021)</a></cite> introduce the concept of task similarity
and demonstrate that orthogonal tasks can be considered independent, implying that they can be executed separately without interfering with each other.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Building on these insights, we define backdoor learning process as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{b},\tau_{c}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{b},\tau_{c}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. By incorporating the subsequent unlearning task <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the overall process of backdoor learning and unlearning can be expressed as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\left\{\tau_{b},\tau_{c},\tau_{u}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\left\{\tau_{b},\tau_{c},\tau_{u}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the orthogonality
between tasks <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can conceptually reorder them for analysis, leading to the following assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="ThmAssumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Assumption 1.</h6>
<div id="ThmAssumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="ThmAssumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="ThmAssumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(Backdoor Learning and Unlearning as Continual Learning).<span id="ThmAssumption1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
The processes of backdoor learning and the subsequent backdoor unlearning can be formalized as a continual learning framework
consisting of a three-stage supervised learning task flow: <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{c},\tau_{b},\tau_{u}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{c},\tau_{b},\tau_{u}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which includes a clean task <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on the clean dataset <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
a backdoor task <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> on the poisoned dataset <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a backdoor unlearning task <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> on the unlearning dataset <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">X</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We provide experimental validation supporting the plausibility of Assumption <a href="#ThmAssumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1. Validating Assumption ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Building on Assumption <a href="#ThmAssumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we formalize backdoor learning and unlearning as a three-stage continual learning
process, which enables us to characterize the backdoor unlearning objective via catastrophic forgetting. Recalling our optimization objective in Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2. Threat Model ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>:
“Effectively and completely eliminate backdoor effects while preserving the model’s performance on clean tasks as much as possible”. Accordingly, we define
Complete Backdoor Unlearning to closely approximate this objective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Definition 0.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(Complete Backdoor Unlearning).<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Let the clean model <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\tau_{c}}^{*}(.;\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau_{c}}^{*}(.;\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is trained solely on the clean task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\tau_{b}}^{*}(.;\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau_{b}}^{*}(.;\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the backdoored model. Based on Assumption <a href="#ThmAssumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the purified model
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\tau_{u}}^{*}(.;\theta_{\tau_{u}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau_{u}}^{*}(.;\theta_{\tau_{u}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as the outcome of a continual learning process that starts from the source clean task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, proceeds
the intermediate backdoor task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, and ultimately reaches the target unlearning task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves complete backdoor unlearning,
if the following conditions hold:</span></span></p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\begin{matrix}\Delta^{\tau_{c}-&gt;\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{c}})=0,\\
\Delta^{\tau_{c}-&gt;\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{b}})=0.\\
\end{matrix}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\begin{matrix}\Delta^{\tau_{c}-&gt;\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{c}})=0,\\
\Delta^{\tau_{c}-&gt;\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{b}})=0.\\
\end{matrix}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">or equivalently,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\begin{matrix}f^{*}_{\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{b}})=f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(X^{\tau_{b}}),\\
f^{*}_{\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{c}})=f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(X^{\tau_{c}}).\\
\end{matrix}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\begin{matrix}f^{*}_{\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{b}})=f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(X^{\tau_{b}}),\\
f^{*}_{\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{c}})=f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(X^{\tau_{c}}).\\
\end{matrix}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A concise interpretation of Definition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> is as follows. Eq. <a href="#S4.E8" title="In Definition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> characterizes complete backdoor
unlearning through its goals: task <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes the forgetting of knowledge from the clean dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> while simultaneously eliminating the backdoor
effect of the poisoned dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In Definition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> offers an equivalent functional view, requiring the purified model
<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\tau_{u}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau_{u}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to match the clean model <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\tau_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> on both clean and poisoned inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let us further analyze the underlying implications of Definition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. According to the characteristics of backdoor attacks,
where “the backdoor model behaves normally on clean datasets, but predicts the poisoned sample into the target label <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>”, we can conclude that for the backdoor
model <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall x_{c}\in X^{\tau_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall x_{c}\in X^{\tau_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(x_{c})=f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(x_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(x_{c})=f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(x_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>. However, for <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall x_{b}\in X^{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall x_{b}\in X^{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(x_{b})=y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(x_{b})=y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which differs from the clean model’s prediction <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(x_{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(x_{b})</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(x_{b})\neq f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(x_{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(x_{b})\neq f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(x_{b})</annotation></semantics></math>.
By incorporating Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In Definition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we obtain</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\begin{matrix}f^{*}_{\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{b}})-f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(X^{\tau_{b}})=-(f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(X^{\tau_{b}})-f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(X^{\tau_{b}}))\neq 0,\\
f^{*}_{\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{c}})-f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(X^{\tau_{c}})=-(f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(X^{\tau_{c}})-f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(X^{\tau_{c}}))=0.\\
\end{matrix}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\begin{matrix}f^{*}_{\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{b}})-f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(X^{\tau_{b}})=-(f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(X^{\tau_{b}})-f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(X^{\tau_{b}}))\neq 0,\\
f^{*}_{\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{c}})-f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(X^{\tau_{c}})=-(f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(X^{\tau_{c}})-f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(X^{\tau_{c}}))=0.\\
\end{matrix}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Eq. <a href="#S4.E10" title="In 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> directly reflects the objective in Eq. <a href="#S4.E8" title="In Definition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Specifically, for the objective <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\tau_{c}-&gt;\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{b}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\tau_{c}-&gt;\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{b}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
the drift induced by the backdoor task <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is compensated by the subsequent unlearning task <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, for the objective <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{\tau_{c}-&gt;\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{c}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{\tau_{c}-&gt;\tau_{u}}(X^{\tau_{c}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
there is no drift at all, implying that the subsequent unlearning task <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> does not interfere with the prior clean task <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> in any way.
As a result, the backdoor knowledge associated with <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> is completely forgotten, while the clean knowledge from <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, we further investigate the conditions that the unlearning task <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> must fulfill to approximately achieve complete backdoor unlearning,
leading to the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Proposition 0.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In a continual learning setting under the NTK regime, consider a three-stage learning task flow: <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{c},\tau_{b},\tau_{u}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{c},\tau_{b},\tau_{u}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which
consists of a clean task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on the dataset <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a backdoor task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a backdoor unlearning task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To approximately achieve complete backdoor unlearning, the task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> should be antagonistic and parallel to the backdoor task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, while remaining
orthogonal to the clean task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, the following conditions should hold:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\begin{matrix}\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)\parallel(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}),\forall x_{u}\in X_{\tau_{u}},\\
\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)\perp(\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}-\theta_{0}),\forall x_{u}\in X_{\tau_{u}},\\
\end{matrix}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>⟂</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\begin{matrix}\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)\parallel(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}),\forall x_{u}\in X_{\tau_{u}},\\
\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)\perp(\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}-\theta_{0}),\forall x_{u}\in X_{\tau_{u}},\\
\end{matrix}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the gradient of the network with respect to the weights <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> at samples from <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is
is the initial model parameter, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}-\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}-\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> approximately denote the gradient directions
associated with the backdoor task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and the clean task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, as interpreted under the NTK linearization.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. see Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1. Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix B Theoretical Proof ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.14078v1/task_similar.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of task similarities within a three-stage learning task flow <math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{c},\tau_{b},\tau_{u}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{\tau_{c},\tau_{b},\tau_{u}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Parameter regions with low error risk indicate areas yielding good task performance.
The vectors <math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the gradient directions associated with backdoor and clean tasks, respectively.
The vector <math id="S4.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as an approximation of <math id="S4.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\tau_{u}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\tau_{u}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
approximating the unlearning direction.
The angles <math id="S4.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> measure the similarity of the backdoor task and the unlearning task to the clean task, respectively.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (1), Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> implies that achieving complete backdoor unlearning requires
constructing the unlearning task <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> to be antagonistic and parallel to the backdoor task <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby maximizing their task similarity and amplifying
catastrophic forgetting of backdoor features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doan et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. At the same time, the task <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> should remain orthogonal to the clean task <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>
so that the drift induced by <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> can be effectively removed without interference with the learned knowledge of <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice, however, the tasks <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> are not always strictly orthogonal, especially for attacks exhibiting low orthogonality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (2), for such cases, the angle <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> falls below <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math>, suggesting a significant overlap or entanglement
between the clean and backdoor tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we consider the adversarial training procedure described in the threat model of Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Threat Model ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> as the unlearning task <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then focus on constructing adversarial examples in a principled manner to approximate the objective of complete backdoor unlearning as effectively as possible,
which leads to Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Proposition 0.</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the context of backdoor adversarial unlearning, the adversarial training procedure can be regarded as the unlearning task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> with dataset <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
following the clean task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and the backdoor task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>. To approximate complete backdoor unlearning, an adversarial example <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}=x+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}=x+\delta</annotation></semantics></math>,
with perturbation <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, should be constructed such that it preserves the true label <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> under the clean model <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> whereas
the backdoor model <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts it as target label <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\tilde{x}\in X^{\tau_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\tilde{x}\in X^{\tau_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(\tilde{x};\theta_{\tau_{c}})=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(\tilde{x};\theta_{\tau_{c}})=y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x};\theta_{\tau_{b}})=y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x};\theta_{\tau_{b}})=y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. see Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2. Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix B Theoretical Proof ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> indicates that complete backdoor unlearning requires the adversarial example <math id="S4.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to exhibit properties
similar to a genuine backdoor sample. Specifically, it should display the backdoor features under the backdoor model <math id="S4.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while preserving
the main semantic features of the original sample <math id="S4.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> when evaluated by the clean model <math id="S4.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the objective remains practically
challenging due to the defender’s lack of access to the poisoned training dataset, as assumed in the threat model in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Threat Model ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Blind Inversion</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As specified in the threat model in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Threat Model ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we now focus on the adversarial risk term <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{adv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math> in the optimization objective,
as formulated in Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2. Threat Model ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{\theta}{\min}~\underset{\delta\in S}{\max}~\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{-y_{t}}}(\ell_{adv}(f(\tilde{x};\theta),y))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜃</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.497em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">S</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{\theta}{\min}~\underset{\delta\in S}{\max}~\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{-y_{t}}}(\ell_{adv}(f(\tilde{x};\theta),y)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">According to Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, to approximate complete backdoor unlearning, the adversarial example distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math>,
generated by the inner optimization of Eq. <a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.2. Blind Inversion ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, should satisfy the conditions:“ <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\tilde{x}\in q(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\tilde{x}\in q(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math>,
①:<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x};\theta_{\tau_{b}})=y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x};\theta_{\tau_{b}})=y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and ②:<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(\tilde{x};\theta_{\tau_{c}})=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}_{\tau_{c}}(\tilde{x};\theta_{\tau_{c}})=y</annotation></semantics></math>”.
To encode condition ① in the optimization process, it is natural to formulate it using cross-entropy loss: <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{ce}(f_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x}),y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{ce}(f_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x}),y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the backdoor model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, since condition ② depends on the clean model <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\tau_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is inherently unknown, the construction of adversarial examples can be naturally
cast as a blind inversion problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2021</a>)</cite>: solving an inverse problem to recover an unknown source from indirect measurements, under the assumption
that the source and measurements are related via a forward model with incomplete or unknown knowledge of parameters.
In this model-based inversion, the adversarial example <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the unobserved sources, and the label distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the
observed measurements. They are related through a forward clean model <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=f_{\tau_{c}}(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=f_{\tau_{c}}(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math> with unknown parameters.
We parameterize the unknown clean model as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and then estimate the true model parameters <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> by optimizing a maximum a posteriori (MAP)
objective, denoted as <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">MAP<sub id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">θ</span></sub></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MAP<sub id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">θ</span></sub></span><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span> This is achieved by optimizing the forward model parameters <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> over the full distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math> of possible adversarial
examples <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> by solving</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\theta^{*}&amp;=\arg\max_{\theta}[\log p(\theta|y)]\\
&amp;=\arg\max_{\theta}\left\{L(q,\theta)+KL(q(\tilde{x})||p(\tilde{x}|\theta,y))\right\}\\
\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>{</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>}</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\theta^{*}&amp;=\arg\max_{\theta}[\log p(\theta|y)]\\
&amp;=\arg\max_{\theta}\left\{L(q,\theta)+KL(q(\tilde{x})||p(\tilde{x}|\theta,y))\right\}\\
\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\begin{matrix}L(q,\theta)=E_{q(\tilde{x})}[log(p(\theta,\tilde{x}|y))]+H(q(\tilde{x}))\\
KL(q(\tilde{x})||p(\tilde{x}|\theta,y))=-\int q(\tilde{x})\log(\frac{p(\tilde{x}|\theta,y)}{q(\tilde{x})})d\tilde{x}.\end{matrix}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\begin{matrix}L(q,\theta)=E_{q(\tilde{x})}[log(p(\theta,\tilde{x}|y))]+H(q(\tilde{x}))\\
KL(q(\tilde{x})||p(\tilde{x}|\theta,y))=-\int q(\tilde{x})\log(\frac{p(\tilde{x}|\theta,y)}{q(\tilde{x})})d\tilde{x}.\end{matrix}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(q,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(q,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the variational lower bound, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(q(\tilde{x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(q(\tilde{x}))</annotation></semantics></math> represents the entropy of the distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{KL}(q(\tilde{x})||p(\tilde{x}|\theta,y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{KL}(q(\tilde{x})||p(\tilde{x}|\theta,y))</annotation></semantics></math>
is the Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence between the distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and the posterior <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\tilde{x}|\theta,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\tilde{x}|\theta,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The derivation can be found in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3. Derivation of MAPθ ‣ Appendix B Theoretical Proof ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Expectation-Maximization (EM) algorithms have long been employed to efficiently solve <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">MAP<sub id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">θ</span></sub></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dempster et al., 1977</a>)</cite>.
This process follows an iterative two-step procedure. For instance, at the <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span>-th iteration:
(1) Expectation step (E-Step): computing the posterior of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> given the current forward model <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>;
(2) Maximization step (M-Step): updating <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> to maximize the expected value of the log-likelihood function with respect to <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expectation Step.</span> E-step aims to learn a distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate the posterior distribution of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, given the current forward
model parameters <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, by optimizing</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.E15.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:60pt;vertical-align:-26.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(36.9pt,-7.1pt) scale(1.31168797013273,1.31168797013273) ;">
<p id="S4.E15.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.E15.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\min_{q(\tilde{x})}KL(q(\tilde{x})||p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)},y))\\
&amp;=\int q(\tilde{x})\log\frac{q(\tilde{x})}{p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)},y)}d\tilde{x}\\
&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{x}\sim q(\tilde{x})}\left[-\log(p(y|\tilde{x},\theta^{(t-1)}))-\log p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)})+\log q(\tilde{x})\right]\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\min_{q(\tilde{x})}KL(q(\tilde{x})||p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)},y))\\
&amp;=\int q(\tilde{x})\log\frac{q(\tilde{x})}{p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)},y)}d\tilde{x}\\
&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{x}\sim q(\tilde{x})}\left[-\log(p(y|\tilde{x},\theta^{(t-1)}))-\log p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)})+\log q(\tilde{x})\right]\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(y|\tilde{x},\theta^{(t-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(y|\tilde{x},\theta^{(t-1)})</annotation></semantics></math> is the data likelihood and <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)})</annotation></semantics></math> is a prior on the generated adversarial examples.
We note that when <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="KL(q(\tilde{x})||p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)},y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">KL(q(\tilde{x})||p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)},y))</annotation></semantics></math> reaches its minimum value of 0, it holds that <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\tilde{x})=p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\tilde{x})=p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)},y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maximization Step.</span> M-step is to use the approximate posterior distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math> from the prior E-step, to update <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, the parameters of
the model, yielding <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is achieved by optimizing <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\theta}L(q,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\theta}L(q,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, while keeping the distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\tilde{x})=p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\tilde{x})=p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)},y)</annotation></semantics></math>
fixed. We have:</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.E16.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:35.6pt;vertical-align:-14.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(37.6pt,-4.3pt) scale(1.31919354686048,1.31919354686048) ;">
<p id="S4.E16.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.E16.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\max_{\theta}L(q,\theta)&amp;=\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{x}\sim q(\tilde{x})}[\log(p(\theta,\tilde{x}|y))]+H(q(\tilde{x}))\\
&amp;=\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{x}\sim q(\tilde{x})}[\log(p(y|\tilde{x},\theta))+\log p(\theta)]+\text{const}\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mtext>const</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\max_{\theta}L(q,\theta)&amp;=\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{x}\sim q(\tilde{x})}[\log(p(\theta,\tilde{x}|y))]+H(q(\tilde{x}))\\
&amp;=\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{x}\sim q(\tilde{x})}[\log(p(y|\tilde{x},\theta))+\log p(\theta)]+\text{const}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is the prior over the model parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Proposed Method</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the Blind Inversion-Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning (BI-BAU) algorithm, which instantiates the adversarial training objective in Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2. Threat Model ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
as a principled EM-style optimization procedure, grounded in Propositions <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inner Maximization.</span> In the E-step, we aim to approximate the distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math> over adversarial examples <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfy the two conditions
outlined in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Blind Inversion ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. Condition ①, requiring <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to trigger the backdoor model, is naturally formulated via
a cross-entropy loss <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{ce}(f_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x}),y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{ce}(f_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x}),y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. Condition ②, enforcing that <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the clean semantics, corresponds to
the data likelihood term <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\log p(y|\tilde{x},\theta^{(t-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log p(y|\tilde{x},\theta^{(t-1)})</annotation></semantics></math> and a prior term <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)})</annotation></semantics></math> that regularizes the adversarial perturbation.
We now elaborate on the two components in light of the discussion in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To instantiate the data likelihood term <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\log p(y|\tilde{x},\theta^{(t-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log p(y|\tilde{x},\theta^{(t-1)})</annotation></semantics></math>, we adopt a knowledge distillation loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al., 2015</a>)</cite> that encourages
<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> to retain the primary semantic features of the original sample <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Unlike conventional knowledge distillation, which transfers knowledge across models
in the parameter space, We instead leverage knowledge distillation as a mechanism to enforce semantic consistency directly in the input space, even under adversarial
perturbations.</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.E17.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:14.7pt;vertical-align:-3.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(28.0pt,-1.3pt) scale(1.21971670919445,1.21971670919445) ;">
<p id="S4.E17.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.E17.m1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ell_{kd}=\alpha\cdot T^{2}\cdot{KL}\big(p_{\theta}(\tilde{x};T)||p_{\tau_{b}}(x;T)\big)+(1-\alpha)\cdot{\ell_{ce}}(f_{\theta}(\tilde{x}),y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{kd}=\alpha\cdot T^{2}\cdot{KL}\big(p_{\theta}(\tilde{x};T)||p_{\tau_{b}}(x;T)\big)+(1-\alpha)\cdot{\ell_{ce}}(f_{\theta}(\tilde{x}),y)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{ce}(f_{\theta}(\tilde{x}),y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{ce}(f_{\theta}(\tilde{x}),y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the cross-entropy loss between the updated model <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and the ground-truth label <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{KL}(p_{\theta}(\tilde{x};T)||p_{\tau_{b}}(x;T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{KL}(p_{\theta}(\tilde{x};T)||p_{\tau_{b}}(x;T))</annotation></semantics></math> measures the discrepancy between the softened outputs of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and the backdoor
model <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the temperature controlling output smoothness, and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> balances the contributions of soft-label (KL) and hard-label (CE) terms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The prior term <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(\tilde{x}|\theta^{(t-1)})</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. <a href="#S4.E15" title="In 4.2. Blind Inversion ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> encodes condition in Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>,
<em id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“<math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)\parallel(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}),\forall x_{u}\in X_{\tau_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)\parallel(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}),\forall x_{u}\in X_{\tau_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>”</em>, which requires that the gradient
of the unlearning task aligns with the backdoor direction to maximally eliminate backdoor effects. Rather than enforcing this condition directly in parameter space,
we realize it in the feature space. We now detail how this transformation is achieved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, based on Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1. Notation ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have</p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial f(x,w)}{\partial w^{l+1}}=\frac{\partial f(x,w)}{\partial h^{l+1}}\cdot\frac{\partial h^{l+1}}{\partial w^{l+1}}=\frac{\partial f(x,w)}{\partial h^{l+1}}\cdot z^{l}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial f(x,w)}{\partial w^{l+1}}=\frac{\partial f(x,w)}{\partial h^{l+1}}\cdot\frac{\partial h^{l+1}}{\partial w^{l+1}}=\frac{\partial f(x,w)}{\partial h^{l+1}}\cdot z^{l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, for the fully connected (FC) classification head that outputs pre-softmax logits and thus does not involve any activation function <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>,
the mapping from <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{l+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{l+1}</annotation></semantics></math> to the output is linear. Consequently, the Jacobian <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial f/\partial h^{l+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial f/\partial h^{l+1}</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to a constant linear mapping.
As a result, the gradient of the output with respect to the FC-layer weights is simply</p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial f(x,w)}{\partial w_{FC}}=z(x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial f(x,w)}{\partial w_{FC}}=z(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{FC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{FC}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the weights of the FC layer and <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(x)=f_{\text{backbone}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>backbone</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(x)=f_{\text{backbone}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the output of the feature extractor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, for a linear FC classification head, the feature representation <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(x)=f_{\text{backbone}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>backbone</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(x)=f_{\text{backbone}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> provides a first-order approximation to the gradient
with respect to the FC weights. Accordingly, we treat the feature perturbation <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(\tilde{x})-z(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(\tilde{x})-z(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as a first-order proxy for the unlearning-task gradient
with respect to the linear classification head.
Let <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\tau_{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\tau_{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> denote the FC-layer weights of the backdoored model and the current model, respectively; the difference <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\tau_{b}}-w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\tau_{b}}-w</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a proxy
for the backdoor gradient direction. To encourage alignment of the adversarial perturbation with the backdoor direction, we maximize the inner product</p>
<table id="S4.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\left\langle z(\tilde{x})-z(x),w_{\tau_{b}}-w\right\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\left\langle z(\tilde{x})-z(x),w_{\tau_{b}}-w\right\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Considering that the clean and backdoor tasks are not strictly orthogonal in practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barni et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>,
as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (2), in such cases, the angle <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> between the backdoor-task gradient and the clean-task gradient direction
<math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically less than <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding angle <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the angle between the unlearning-task gradient and the clean-task gradient direction <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which could be approximated by the angle between the perturbation <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(\tilde{x})-z(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(\tilde{x})-z(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and the clean feature <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. This approximation allows us to balance
the alignment with the backdoor direction while preserving the semantic integrity of clean features. Accordingly, the prior loss is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{prior}=\left\langle z(\tilde{x})-z(x),w_{\tau_{b}}-w\right\rangle-\cos(\beta+k\cdot\frac{\pi}{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{prior}=\left\langle z(\tilde{x})-z(x),w_{\tau_{b}}-w\right\rangle-\cos(\beta+k\cdot\frac{\pi}{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\approx\arccos\left(\frac{\left\langle z(\tilde{x})-z(x),z(x)\right\rangle}{\left|z(\tilde{x})-z(x)\right|\cdot\left|z(x)\right|}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\approx\arccos\left(\frac{\left\langle z(\tilde{x})-z(x),z(x)\right\rangle}{\left|z(\tilde{x})-z(x)\right|\cdot\left|z(x)\right|}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> controls the magnitude of the angle <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, when <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{prior}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{prior}</annotation></semantics></math> is maximized at <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\pi/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring the unlearning task
is orthogonal to the clean task and minimally interferes with it. Conversely, <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0.0</annotation></semantics></math> aligns <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> fully with backdoor features, at the cost of clean semantics.
Thus, <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> balances backdoor alignment and clean feature preservation. The effect of <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> on the robustness of BI-BAU is analyzed in the Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2. Investigation of the Hyperparameter 𝑘 in the Prior Loss ℓ_{𝑝⁢𝑟⁢𝑖⁢𝑜⁢𝑟} ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Combining conditions ① and ②, we have the adversarial loss for the inner optimization as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{adv-in}=-\ell_{ce}(f_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x}),y_{t})-\lambda_{1}\ell_{kd}+\lambda_{2}\ell_{prior}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{adv-in}=-\ell_{ce}(f_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x}),y_{t})-\lambda_{1}\ell_{kd}+\lambda_{2}\ell_{prior}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are trade-off weights that balance the preservation of semantic features of the original sample and the strength of
the adversarial perturbation, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Outer Minimization.</span> In the M-step, we update the model parameters <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> using the adversarial example distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math> from the E-step to
purify the backdoor model. This is achieved by optimizing <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\theta}L(q,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\theta}L(q,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, which involves the likelihood term <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(y\mid\tilde{x},\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(y\mid\tilde{x},\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>
as well as the prior distribution term <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. <a href="#S4.E16" title="In 4.2. Blind Inversion ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>.
The likelihood term is implemented via cross-entropy, <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{ce}(f_{\theta}(\tilde{x};\theta),y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{ce}(f_{\theta}(\tilde{x};\theta),y)</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring that <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is labeled
with its true class to fine-tune the model and improve robustness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The prior term <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> enforces the condition of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>,
<em id="S4.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“<math id="S4.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{c},\theta)\perp(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⟂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{c},\theta)\perp(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>”</em>,
which requires that the clean task <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> to be orthogonal to the backdoor task <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Equivalently, this condition can be expressed as minimizing the Euclidean inner product <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{c},\theta),\theta_{\tau_{b}}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{c},\theta),\theta_{\tau_{b}}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>,
which aligns with the concept of Elastic Weight Consolidation (EWC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirkpatrick et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, a technique commonly used to alleviate catastrophic forgetting in neural networks.
Since the clean model is unavailable, we replace <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with the current model parameter <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding</p>
<table id="S4.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(23)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{ewc}=(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta)^{T}F_{\tau_{c}}(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{ewc}=(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta)^{T}F_{\tau_{c}}(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\tau_{c}}=\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log p(X^{\tau_{c}}|\theta)\nabla_{\theta}\log p(X^{\tau_{c}}|\theta)^{T}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\tau_{c}}=\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log p(X^{\tau_{c}}|\theta)\nabla_{\theta}\log p(X^{\tau_{c}}|\theta)^{T}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> is the Fisher Information Matrix (FIM)
for the clean dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The term <math id="S4.SS3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{ewc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{ewc}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that the model’s performance on the clean task is further preserved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 0. ‣ 4.1. Problem Formulation ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> implies that “<math id="S4.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)\parallel(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}f_{0}(x_{u},\theta)\parallel(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta_{\tau_{c}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>”,
indicating that the unlearning task <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> should be antagonistic and parallel to the backdoor task <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, for unlearning task <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we adopt the inverse of the EWC loss, clipped to <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[0,1.0\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[0,1.0\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding:</p>
<table id="S4.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(24)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{inv-ewc}=1.0/\left[1.0+(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta)^{T}F_{\tau_{u}}(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{inv-ewc}=1.0/\left[1.0+(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta)^{T}F_{\tau_{u}}(\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*}-\theta)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p11.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\tau_{u}}=\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log p(X^{\tau_{u}}|\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*})\nabla_{\theta}\log p(X^{\tau_{u}}|\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*})^{T}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\tau_{u}}=\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log p(X^{\tau_{u}}|\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*})\nabla_{\theta}\log p(X^{\tau_{u}}|\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*})^{T}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> is
the Fisher Information Matrix (FIM) computed on the unlearning dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\tau_{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\tau_{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> under the backdoored model <math id="S4.SS3.p11.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\tau_{b}}^{*}(.;\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tau_{b}}^{*}(.;\theta_{\tau_{b}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. This design
encourages parameter updates to follow the backdoor gradient direction, thereby promoting effective forgetting of backdoor-related features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the outer optimization loss is:</p>
<table id="S4.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(25)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{adv-out}=\ell_{ce}(f_{\theta}(\tilde{x};\theta),y)+\gamma_{1}\ell_{inv-ewc}+\gamma_{2}\ell_{ewc}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{adv-out}=\ell_{ce}(f_{\theta}(\tilde{x};\theta),y)+\gamma_{1}\ell_{inv-ewc}+\gamma_{2}\ell_{ewc}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p12.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are trade-off weights that balance effective backdoor unlearning and the preservation of clean-task performance, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall objective.</span> In summary, combining Equations <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2. Threat Model ‣ 3. Preliminary ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>,  <a href="#S4.E22" title="In 4.3. Proposed Method ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> and <a href="#S4.E25" title="In 4.3. Proposed Method ‣ 4. Methodology ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>, we propose the bi-level optimization objective:</p>
<table id="S4.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(26)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.E26.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:327.8pt;height:37.1pt;vertical-align:-15.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(48.1pt,-5.4pt) scale(1.41585458024913,1.41585458024913) ;">
<p id="S4.E26.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.E26.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\min_{\theta}~\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{cl}}\left[\ell(x,y;\theta)\right]+\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{-y_{t}}}\left[\mathcal{L}_{adv-out}(\tilde{x},y;\theta)\right]\\
&amp;s.t.~~~\delta^{*}=\arg~\max_{\delta\in S}~\mathcal{L}_{adv-in}(\tilde{x},y_{t},y;\theta).\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.827em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="1.487em">.</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.827em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.497em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\min_{\theta}~\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{cl}}\left[\ell(x,y;\theta)\right]+\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{-y_{t}}}\left[\mathcal{L}_{adv-out}(\tilde{x},y;\theta)\right]\\
&amp;s.t.~~~\delta^{*}=\arg~\max_{\delta\in S}~\mathcal{L}_{adv-in}(\tilde{x},y_{t},y;\theta).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Extension to Untargeted Adversarial Training.</span> In the case where the target label <math id="S4.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown, for the inner optimization problem,
we adopt untargeted adversarial examples. Consequently, we define the adversarial loss as <math id="S4.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{adv-in}=\ell_{ce}(f_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x}),y)-\lambda_{1}\ell_{kd}+\lambda_{2}\ell_{prior}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{adv-in}=\ell_{ce}(f_{\tau_{b}}(\tilde{x}),y)-\lambda_{1}\ell_{kd}+\lambda_{2}\ell_{prior}</annotation></semantics></math>
and formulate the overall optimization objective as:</p>
<table id="S4.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(27)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.E27.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:293.3pt;height:42.3pt;vertical-align:-17.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(56.0pt,-8.1pt) scale(1.61831785824535,1.61831785824535) ;">
<p id="S4.E27.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.E27.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\min_{\theta}~\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{cl}}\left[\ell(x,y;\theta)+\mathcal{L}_{adv-out}(\tilde{x},y;\theta)\right]\\
&amp;s.t.~~~\delta^{*}=\arg~\max_{\delta\in S}~\mathcal{L}_{adv-in}(\tilde{x},y;\theta).\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.827em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="1.487em">.</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.827em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.497em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\min_{\theta}~\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}_{cl}}\left[\ell(x,y;\theta)+\mathcal{L}_{adv-out}(\tilde{x},y;\theta)\right]\\
&amp;s.t.~~~\delta^{*}=\arg~\max_{\delta\in S}~\mathcal{L}_{adv-in}(\tilde{x},y;\theta).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Extension to Multi-Modal Contrastive Learning.</span>
The extension of BI-BAU to multi-modal contrastive learning settings is presented in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Extending BI-BAU to Multi-Modal Contrastive Learning ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experiment Settings</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our proposed BI-BAU method under two learning scenarios: <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">supervised learning</em> and <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multi-modal contrastive learning</em>, considering
diverse datasets, model architectures, and attack types. Detailed descriptions of the datasets and network architectures are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1. Datasets and Network Architectures ‣ Appendix D Additional Implementation Details ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervised learning.</span> For standard image classification tasks, we conduct all attack and defense tasks on three benchmark datasets: CIFAR-10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky et al., 2009</a>)</cite>,
Tiny-ImageNet-200 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, and GTSRB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stallkamp et al., 2011</a>)</cite>, using three neural network architectures: ResNet-18 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, VGG19 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Simonyan and Zisserman, 2014</a>)</cite>
and Vision Transformer (ViT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Additional experiments on diverse datasets and models, including Tiny-ImageNet-200 and VGG19, are reported in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3. Additional Experiments on Diverse Datasets and Models ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Modal Contrastive Learning.</span> For multi-modal evaluation, we adopt OpenAI’s open-source CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2021</a>)</cite> as the pre-trained backbone,
trained on 400 million image-text pairs. Specifically, the “ViT-B/32” variant serves as the visual encoder and a Transformer as the text encoder. We first
implant a backdoor into a pre-trained CLIP model on ImageNet-1K. Then, we construct a fine-tuning dataset by sampling 50,000 image-text pairs from
Conceptual Captions (CC3M) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, corresponding to only  0.6% of the full dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Baselines.</span> For supervised learning, we conduct an extensive investigation and adopt a wide range of representative backdoor attacks to evaluate
our approach, categorized as follows: (1) Patch-based backdoor attacks: BadNets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and Blended <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2017</a>)</cite>; (2) Invisible backdoor attacks:
WaNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen and Tran, 2021</a>)</cite> and Refool <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2020</a>)</cite>; (3) Sample-specific backdoor attacks: IAD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen and Tran, 2020</a>)</cite> and ISSBA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>;
(4) Clean-label attacks: SIG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barni et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and Narcissus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. We further include adaptive attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>
designed to circumvent latent separation-based defenses, such as Adap-Blend and Adap-Patch. For multi-modal contrastive learning, we evaluate attack baselines,
include BadNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and the state-of-the-art (SoTA) BadCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. BadCLIP is specifically designed for multi-modal model and exhibits
strong resilience against existing defense mechanisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we implement the backdoor re-activation attacks proposed by Min <em id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">et al.</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, including Query-based Re-activation Attack (QRA)
and Retuning Attack (RA), to verify that our proposed BI-BAU method can effectively and thoroughly eliminate backdoor effects in backdoor models. Detailed attack
configurations are provided in the Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2. Attack Settings ‣ Appendix D Additional Implementation Details ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Defense Baselines.</span> For supervised learning, we evaluate the effectiveness of our proposed BI-BAU method by comparing it with state-of-the-art post-purification
approaches, including: (1) trigger synthesis based approaches, such as NC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and BTI-DBF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; (2) model reconstruction based
approaches, including ANP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu and Wang, 2021</a>)</cite>, EP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, RNP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and CLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2022a</a>)</cite>; (3) backdoor adversarial
unlearning approaches, such as I-BAU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and SAU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. We further compare with the recently proposed PAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2024</a>)</cite>
by Min <em id="S5.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">et al.</em> to highlight the its advantages. For multi-modal contrastive learning, we adopt CleanCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bansal et al., 2023</a>)</cite> as a baseline defense for comparison.
In addition, as a comparative reference, we construct an idealized backdoor unlearning scenario (termed IBU), in which we assume that the defender has access to
the real backdoor samples along with a small subset of clean data, and performs fine-tuning to remove the backdoor from the model.
Further defense settings are provided in the Appendix <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3. Defense Settings ‣ Appendix D Additional Implementation Details ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span> By default, all defense methods have access to <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\%</annotation></semantics></math> of clean training data, with evaluation metrics including Clean Accuracy (CA)
and Attack Success Rate (ASR), where R-ASR and Q-ASR represent ASR after performing Retuning Attack (RA) and Query-based Re-activation Attack (QRA), respectively.
We further verify the thoroughness of backdoor removal by measuring the Backdoor Existence Coefficient (BEC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> at the neural level.
The implementation details of BEC are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS4" title="D.4. Layer-wise Activation Analysis ‣ Appendix D Additional Implementation Details ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a>. <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            In the following tables, we use 
<span id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:14.6pt;height:7.5pt;vertical-align:-1.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-2.4pt,1.3pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<span id="footnote1.2.1" class="ltx_p">[<span id="footnote1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFCCCC;">red</span>]</span>
</span></span>
to mark some cases where the defense is ineffective, as indicated by a persistently high ASR, and 
<span id="footnote1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:21.7pt;height:7.5pt;vertical-align:-1.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-3.6pt,1.3pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<span id="footnote1.3.1" class="ltx_p">[<span id="footnote1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D9F2D9;">green</span>]</span>
</span></span> to highlight
the best-performing result among the six baseline methods. “BI-BAU (T)” and “BI-BAU (U)” refer to the defense performance under targeted and untargeted attack
settings, respectively.</span></span></span> In addition, a comparison of the efficiency of the defense methods is presented in Section <a href="#S5.SS8" title="5.8. Efficiency Evaluation ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Rethinking Backdoor Adversarial Unlearning through the Lens of Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance (%) of safety tuning strategies for eliminating backdoor effects on ResNet-18 (CIFAR-10).</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:338.1pt;height:56pt;vertical-align:-27.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-318.8pt,52.8pt) scale(0.346490532405834,0.346490532405834) ;">
<table id="S5.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">Attack <math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;" colspan="2">BadNets</td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;" colspan="2">Blended</td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;" colspan="2">WaNet</td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;" colspan="2">Refool</td>
<td id="S5.T1.5.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;" colspan="2">IAD</td>
